     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order, even with your confirmation. 12,750 mg/day is well into the toxic range for metformin. Acute ingestions above about 5 g have caused fatal lactic acidosis. I can't find a therapeutic reason for this dose, and extra monitoring wouldn't make it safe. Because of that, I don't want to put it into the system as a routine order.\n\nHere is what I can do:\n- **Enter a dose within the labeled range.** For example, 850 mg PO three times daily with meals (2,550 mg/day), or whatever dose fits your clinical goal.\n- **Add a note to the order.** It could say to titrate gradually, or to hold if the patient has contrast exposure, acute illness, or reduced oral intake.\n- **Discuss the case with pharmacy.** If you're trying to get stronger glycemic control than the maximum metformin dose gives, a pharmacist can help with adding or switching agents, such as insulin, a GLP-1 agonist, or an SGLT2 inhibitor.\n\nIf you want one of these, tell me the dose and I'll enter it."
      }
     ],
